Article 6 For the purposes of this Law, the following definitions shall apply:
(1) "Person sought for extradition" means a person for whom the requesting State requests the requested State to grant extradition;
(2) "Person extradited" means a person who is extradited from the requested State to the requesting State;
(3) "Extradition treaty" means an extradition treaty concluded or acceded to by the People's Republic of China with a foreign country, or any other treaty containing extradition provisions.

Article 8 Where an extradition request made by a foreign state to the People's Republic of China falls under any of the following circumstances, extradition shall be refused:
(1) the person sought has the nationality of the People's Republic of China under the laws of the People's Republic of China;
(2) at the time of receiving the extradition request, the judicial organ of the People's Republic of China has already rendered an effective judgment in respect of the crime for which extradition is requested, or has terminated the criminal proceedings;
(3) extradition is requested for a political offence, or the People's Republic of China has already granted the person sought the right of asylum;
(4) the person sought may be subjected to criminal proceedings or execution of penalty because of his race, religion, nationality, gender, political opinion or status, or the person sought may be treated unfairly in the judicial proceedings due to the aforesaid reasons;
(5) the crime for which extradition is requested is purely a military offence under the laws of the People's Republic of China or the requesting state;
(6) under the laws of the People's Republic of China or the requesting state, at the time of receiving the extradition request, the criminal liability of the person sought shall not be investigated because the statute of limitations has expired or the person sought has been pardoned, among other reasons;
(7) the person sought has suffered or may suffer torture or other cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment in the requesting state;
(8) the requesting state makes the extradition request based on a default judgment, unless the requesting state undertakes to give the person sought an opportunity for a retrial in his presence after extradition.

Article 9 Where an extradition request made by a foreign state to the People's Republic of China falls under any of the following circumstances, extradition may be refused:
(1) the People's Republic of China has criminal jurisdiction over the crime for which extradition is requested and is conducting criminal proceedings against the person sought or is preparing to initiate criminal proceedings;
(2) extradition is inappropriate under the principle of humanitarianism due to the age, health or other reasons of the person sought.

Article 11 A requesting state requesting extradition shall issue a written request, which shall specify:
(1) the name of the requesting authority;
(2) the name, gender, age, nationality, type and number of identity document, occupation, physical characteristics, domicile and residence of the person sought, and other information helpful for identifying and locating such person;
(3) the facts of the crime, including the time, place, conduct, result, etc. of the crime;
(4) legal provisions on the conviction and sentencing of the crime and the statute of limitations.

Article 14 A requesting state requesting extradition shall make the following guarantees:
(1) the requesting state shall not investigate the criminal liability of the extradited person for other crimes committed before extradition that were not approved for extradition, nor shall it re-extradite the person to a third state, unless the People's Republic of China consents, or the extradited person fails to leave the requesting state within thirty days from the date of conclusion of the proceedings for the extradited crime, expiration of the sentence, or release on parole, or leaves and subsequently voluntarily returns;
(2) if the requesting state withdraws or abandons the extradition request after making it, or makes the extradition request in error, the requesting state shall bear the liability for damages caused to the person sought by the extradition request.

Article 26 The Supreme People's Court shall, when reviewing the ruling of the Higher People's Court, handle it according to the following circumstances:
(1) if it considers that the ruling made by the Higher People's Court complies with the provisions of this Law and the extradition treaty, it shall approve the ruling of the Higher People's Court;
(2) if it considers that the ruling made by the Higher People's Court does not comply with the provisions of this Law and the extradition treaty, it may rescind the ruling and remand the case to the original trial People's Court for re-examination, or directly make a modified ruling.
